e i think as  gail pointed out a lot of ceos say that  they want to increase diversity in their  companies but they very rarely ever  interact with anybody who's not like  them because we all sometimes live in  our bubble what was that like for you  well i'll step back so  early on in 2019 i actually spent time  with a group of my black officers we  went to atlanta together and i've been  doing these what i call immersion trips  for five or six years now  bring eight or ten leaders together i  travel with them for a day introduce  them to other ceos  business leaders in the community and we  get to know each other  and in january of 2019 i went to atlanta  with a group of black officers and when  we finished up the session they said  brian there are team members in  minneapolis that would love this  experience i said well let's set it up  and  they said would you go to this barber  shop  in minneapolis and we'll meet with about  40 team members there  so it was houston's barbershop but i had  a chance to just spend time with my  local black team members and just get to  know them understand some of the  challenges they were facing things that  were on their mind but just spend that  time connecting yeah and for me it  actually prepared me for  george foy's murder  because the first thing i did  coming out of that was recognize i had  to connect with my black leaders and i  will always remember gail  having a zoom call  right after george boy's murder  and seeing a screen after screen after  screen on the zoom call  of black leaders with tears in their  eyes talking about the challenges they  have been facing and the tough  conversations they had had with  family members their kids it was a very  painful conversation  but they helped inspire all of us to say  we've got to stand up and do more well  there's nothing like a black barber shop  to go and hear the truth that's true  and speaking of here that hearing the  truth i i want to turn attention to the  economy uh 